[Arithmos & Metric Collection | VDisk & Virtual Disk handling in Arithmos]
## Example
Let’s suppose that we run on ESX (non-KVM) and have the following virtual disk entities in the database (everything is scoped to the node):
{ id=”123”, ... }
 { id=”253”, ... }
 { id=”842”, ... }
Now suppose AgentSendStats is called with the following virtual disk stats:
{ id=”123”, ... }
 { id=”453”, ... }
 { id=”842”, ... }
After the input stats are processed, the database state looks like this:
{ id=”123”, ... }
 { id=”253”, ... }
 { id=”453”, ... }
 { id=”842”, ... }
And valid_entity_id_set has the following data: “123”, “453”, “842”.  Then ScanVirtualDisksAndMarkToDelete(valid_entity_id_set) is called, which iterates over database entities and marks for deletion entities that are not in valid_entity_id_set.  After it finishes, the database looks like this:
{ id=”123”, ... }
 { id=”253”, ... }
 { id=”453”, ... }
 { id=”842”, ... }
The virtual disk entity object with id=”123” is marked for deletion because it’s not in the valid_entity_id_set.

[Arithmos & Metric Collection | VDisk & Virtual Disk handling in Arithmos]
## Example
Let’s suppose that we have the following Virtual Disk entities in the database (everything is scoped to the node):
{ id=”123”, disk_extents_nutanix_vdisk_name=”NFS:1001,NFS:1006”, ... }
 { id=”253”, disk_extents_nutanix_vdisk_name=”NFS:1043”, ... }
 { id=”842”, disk_extents_nutanix_vdisk_name=”NFS:1020”, ... }
and the following VDisk entities:
{ id=”NFS:1001”, … }
 { id=”NFS:1043”, … }
Once the NFS resolution finishes, the accepted_vdisk_name_resolver_map_ would have the following entries (that’s driven by the Virtual Disks that we have and their NFS paths):
[ “NFS:1001” ⇒ …, “NFS:1006” ⇒ …, “NFS:1020” ⇒ …, “NFS:1043” ⇒ … ]
Now suppose, AgentSendStats is called with the following VDisk stats:
{ id=”12323”, vdisk_name=”NFS:1001”, …}
 { id=”34523”, vdisk_name=”NFS:1098”, …}
 { id=”77323”, vdisk_name=”NFS:1006”, …}
The { id=”34523”, vdisk_name=”NFS:1098”, …} gets ignored, because it’s not in the accepted_vdisk_name_resolver_map_.
After the remaining stats are processed, the VDisk entities will look like this:
{ id=”NFS:1001”, … }
 { id=”NFS:1006”, … }
 { id=”NFS:1043”, … }
Note that Arithmos uses vdisk_name as id.  Also note, that even though NFS:1043 wasn’t present in the stats, it wasn’t deleted: the deletion would happen (in non-KVM case) when the corresponding Virtual Disk entity is deleted.

