[MongoDB Integration | Problem with query operator conversion for filtering]
The fundamental issue is for different operators, IDF deal with the list value differently. For example, for {foo kEQ 1}, IDF will ignore list value. But for {foo kGE 1}, IDF will return both singular and list value. This document will discuss these discrepancies which should be fixed.

## kEQ
db.inventory.insertMany([
{ item: "e1", qty: 50, m1: ["red", "blank"], m2: [ 11 ] },
{ item: "e2", qty: 100, m1: ["red", "blank", "plain"], m2: 11 },
{ item: "e3", qty: 75, m1: ["blank", "red"], m2: [ 11, 12 ] }
{ item: "e4", qty: 75, m1: ["blank", "red"], m2: [ 12, 11 ] }
]);
TL:DR (Query: m2 kEQ 11)
The problem here is if the query filters on singular value, IDF will match only singular value while Mongo matches also list value. So we need to add additional constraints to the filter to get rid of list value in this case.
db.inventory.aggregate({$match: {
{$and: [
{"m2": {$not: {$type: "array"}}},
{"m2": {$eq: 11}}
]}
}})
{ "_id" : ObjectId("5e0a81bf3ba27cdd85c0fa28"), "item" : "id3", "qty" : 100, "tags" : [ "red", "blank", "plain" ], "m2" : 11 }
For list $eq list, it has been solved by $size + $all.

